                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game is really rough around the edges: the English writing is clunky, typos are plenty even 4 months after the release, some scenes are questionable at best, both the humouristic and violent ones. Some side characters and plot threads are so irrelevant that it's hard to say why they're even there. Choices also are absolutely pointless, not even giving an illusion that you're influencing the story. The conclusion of the story is really vague, outside of the main couple being together.\n\nOn the other hand, the core ideas behind the story and the few characters that matter are pretty fun. It's a very straightforward power fantasy with romance and unlike this studio's previous game, Episicava, it's not overly edgy and avoids unnecessary contrivance. There's relatively little tension, the protagonist gradually becoming a literal God, but the beautiful art and lighthearted banter between him and the heroine, Myra, make for some relaxing, mindless fun. There are also two relatively competent h-scenes that show up in reasonable moments and add to the romance subplot. The soundtrack is pretty upbeat, underlining the lighter tone of the game, but it also features some more intense tunes for battle segments and generally works reasonably well.\n\nI backed this game on Kickstarter mostly out of curiosity, wondering whether this studio will improve with their new projects and they definitely did. Rainbow Dreams is not anything amazing, but it's a good-enough way to burn a few hours and if you're not expecting tons of narrative depth, I think it's worth the asking price. And with a bit more polish and attention to detail, it could've been genuinely good... I wonder what Epic Works will come up with next time.\n\n-------------\n\nPS If you're interested in quality visual novels, check out [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/32980295/]my curator page[/url].",
